Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below are the columns that matter for this task and how they are going to be used  

• `geo_id` : the tract FIPS code – will be used to JOIN the two tables and to identify California tracts (`geo_id` starts with “06”).  
• `median_income` : the 5‑year ACS median household income in each tract – will be compared between 2015 and 2018.  

Optional filter / helper columns  

• `do_date` (in 2018 table) : not strictly necessary, but can be kept if you want to be sure you only look at the 2018‑vintage estimates.  
• Any other column can be used to sanity–check, but is not required for the calculation.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ten BigQuery queries (simple → complex) that progressively lead to the final answer.
All tables live in the same project/dataset:

```
`bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr`
`bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr`
```

Every query is annotated and capped with `LIMIT 20`.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few 2015 rows: tract code and median income
SELECT geo_id, median_income
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at a few 2018 rows: tract code, do_date stamp and median income
SELECT geo_id, do_date, median_income
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct 2015 California tract codes
SELECT DISTINCT geo_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr`
WHERE geo_id LIKE '06%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Distinct 2018 California tract codes
SELECT DISTINCT geo_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr`
WHERE geo_id LIKE '06%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join 2015 & 2018 on California tracts, keep both median incomes
SELECT
  t18.geo_id,
  t15.median_income AS median_income_2015,
  t18.median_income AS median_income_2018
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
  ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same join plus a computed increase (2018 – 2015)
SELECT
  t18.geo_id,
  t15.median_income AS median_income_2015,
  t18.median_income AS median_income_2018,
  (t18.median_income - t15.median_income) AS income_change
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
  ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. California tracts ordered by biggest gains, still showing top 20
SELECT
  t18.geo_id,
  (t18.median_income - t15.median_income) AS income_change
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
  ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
  AND t15.median_income IS NOT NULL
  AND t18.median_income IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY income_change DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same idea but also display the absolute 2015 and 2018 figures
SELECT
  t18.geo_id,
  t15.median_income AS median_income_2015,
  t18.median_income AS median_income_2018,
  (t18.median_income - t15.median_income) AS income_change
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
  ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
  AND t15.median_income IS NOT NULL
  AND t18.median_income IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY income_change DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Use a common table expression (CTE) to make the difference, then rank
WITH ca_income_change AS (
  SELECT
    t18.geo_id,
    t15.median_income AS median_income_2015,
    t18.median_income AS median_income_2018,
    (t18.median_income - t15.median_income) AS income_change
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
    ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
  WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
    AND t15.median_income IS NOT NULL
    AND t18.median_income IS NOT NULL
)
SELECT *
FROM ca_income_change
ORDER BY income_change DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: return the single California census tract with the largest income increase
SELECT
  t18.geo_id AS tract_code_with_largest_gain,
  (t18.median_income - t15.median_income) AS income_change
FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2015_5yr` AS t15
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.censustract_2018_5yr` AS t18
  ON t15.geo_id = t18.geo_id
WHERE t15.geo_id LIKE '06%'
  AND t15.median_income IS NOT NULL
  AND t18.median_income IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY income_change DESC
LIMIT 1;     -- remove or raise the limit if you want to double‑check the runner‑ups
```

